<header><p class="st-kicker">khanate · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Kumul Khanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1696 CE – 1930 CE · capital Hami</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A semi-autonomous Uyghur principality at Hami, loyal to the Qing for over two centuries, whose abolition in 1930 set off a major revolt in Xinjiang.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Muslim ruling family of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hami">Hami</a> oasis, threatened by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/dzungar-khanate">Dzungar Khanate</a>, submitted to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kangxi-emperor">Kangxi Emperor</a> in 1696 and helped Qing armies against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzungars">Dzungars</a>. Its leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-allah-beg">Abd Allah Beg</a> was rewarded with a hereditary rank, and his descendants became princes (wang) of Hami under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/qing-empire">Qing Empire</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The khans governed the Turkic Muslim inhabitants of the oasis through their own begs, collected taxes in kind and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corvee-labour">corvée</a> from their subjects, and supplied the Qing with troops and provisions. They kept their position through the Qing conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a> in 1759 and the turmoil of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/yettishar">Yettishar</a>, and were regarded as one of the most loyal frontier houses.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the death of the last khan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maqsud-shah">Maqsud Shah</a>, in 1930, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a> governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jin-shuren">Jin Shuren</a> abolished the khanate and imposed direct rule and new taxes. The resulting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hami">Kumul</a> rebellion of 1931 drew in the Hui warlord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ma-zhongying">Ma Zhongying</a> and helped spark the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/first-east-turkestan-republic">First East Turkestan Republic</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kumul-khanate.jpg" alt="Kumul Khanate" width="320" height="272"><figcaption>Image: Joowwww · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:China_Xinjiang_Hami.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1696 CE – 1930 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>khanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Hami</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Abd Allah Beg, Maqsud Shah</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Uyghur (Chagatai)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hami Khanate</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1813&amp;focus=kumul-khanate" aria-label="Kumul Khanate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6940 656 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kumul Khanate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7260 912L7567 1068"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7567 1068h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7260 912h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7260 912h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Hami. <a href="/atlas?year=1813&amp;focus=kumul-khanate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

